00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
look at the biggest accomplishments the  setbacks and the challenges during  president joe biden's first year in  office today is the anniversary and the  president gave himself high marks at his  news conference yesterday afternoon  saying he quote  probably outperformed what anybody  thought would happen now one year ago  today mr biden was sworn in after a very  contentious election that divided the  country and amid a pandemic that has now  dominated american life and continues to  do so unfortunately and o'keeffe shows  us all that has happened over the last  365 days  my whole soul is in this  bringing america together  uniting our people during his first  speech as commander-in-chief president  biden promised to tackle the nation's  many challenges  but unity quickly proved hard to find  racial justice protests continued  while teachers parents and students  bristled plans to keep schools safe from  the coronavirus the new administration  ramped up vaccine distribution and  passed trillions of dollars in financial  relief we're closer than ever to  declaring our independence from this  deadly virus over the summer the  pandemic took a temporary back seat to  foreign policy as a controversial  decision to end america's involvement in  afghanistan sparked chaos i was not  going to extend this forever war and i  was not extending a forever exit here we  go the administration succeeded in  enacting major infrastructure  legislation let's believe in  possibilities let's believe in one  another but plans for an ambitious  spending bill remained stalled as fall  turned to winter supply chain  disruptions and inflation and consumer  prices soaring and a new covet variant  sparked long lines at testing and  vaccination sites once again we have to  do more we have to do better and we will  still the president kicked off the new  year pushing for voting reform and  blasting his predecessor's supporters  they want chaos to reign  we want the people to rule and unity in  congress like in much of the country  continues to remain elusive for cbs  mornings i'm ed o'keefe at the white  house  for more on what happened and what it  means we are joined now by cbs news  political contributor and democratic  strategist joel payne and steve hayes he  is co-founder ceo and editor of the  dispatch gentlemen good morning to both  of you uh that was a nearly two-hour  long press conference yesterday there  were questions from conservative outlets  liberal outlets middle of the road  outlets foreign language outlets  international outlets if it went any  longer dogs and children were going to  be asking questions so if you could joel  off the top help us take away kind of  the headline what was the big message  that you got from those two hours  well i think some of the message was  non-verbal which is i'm the president i  can stand and take the heat and i've got  the endurance to do the job and i think  that's part of what his team wanted to  do they wanted to reflect that you have  an engaged president you have a  president that is focused on the job at  hand  he did make some news for his team  unfortunately they had to do some  cleanup afterwards related to  issues in russia  he made some comments about  potentially russia invading the ukraine  which his team again had to clean up  afterwards and i think he also made news  around the buildback better agenda  revealing that that is going to have to  be broken up into multiple pieces of  legislation so you know press  conferences like that they they really  don't move the needle i think that they  more reflect the direction and i think  the direction of this president is to  attempt to reset as we look at the  beginning of 2022. yeah steve  the president in his inaugural address  uh promised to be a unifier for the  country he campaigned that way as well  uh unfortunately for the administration  people seem to be unifying around  disapproval at the moment do you think  that press conference changed the  narrative in any way  i don't i mean i agree with joel that  you know if endurance was one of the  measuring sticks he did well i mean i  think people were surprised that he was  able to stand up there for two hours and  answer questions but i think he raised  as many questions as he answered in in  several different ways i mean if you  talk about look at the his comment about  a minor incursion by the russians into  ukraine  you saw immediate reaction from overseas  particularly in kiev where officials  said boy that seems like a green light  for vladimir putin you also had the  president's comments on  uh the possibility of an illegitimate  election in 2022 when he was asked a  question about whether if these voting  reforms that have been debated in  congress don't pass that would  contribute to the illegitimacy of the  2022 elections i think the right answer  would be to reaffirm  the strength of our electoral system  while you know expressing disappointment  that that the the reforms were unlikely  to pass instead he said yeah it's  possible that the 2022 elections could  be illegitimate which i think is not  what we need whether you're a republican  or a democrat a liberal or conservative  i know so steve how is that helpful and  doesn't that set up a tit-for-tat  dynamic that i think is very troubling  on many issues  yeah i mean i think it's worrisome i  mean look if you're a conservative and  you've got questions or problems with  the way that donald trump conducted  himself in in the aftermath of the 2020  election lying about whether the  election was stolen trying to remain in  office what his supporters have been  doing in states and localities across  the country  um to to basically put themselves in a  position where they could muscle through  uh an illegitimate election the last  thing you wanted to hear was to hear  from president biden that there are  questions about the legitimacy of the  elections you needed the president to  say no we've got the best electoral  system in the world  the safest the most secure and one thing  we don't need to worry about even if i'm  frustrated that republicans aren't  supporting me on these voting reforms  the one thing that we can be sure of is  that the elections will be legitimate  joel you mentioned it briefly the  president said that he is considering  breaking up the build back better plan  is it as simple as just giving in and  trying to pass it in chunks as you said  well here's the thing i mean part of  that would be you'd still need to get 60  votes for some of that now there are  parts of that agenda that if you maybe  offered up piecemeal you could maybe get  to that 60 vote threshold you got folks  like senator romney who might support  parts of that agenda you've got other  moderates in that republican caucus who  might support part of that agenda and i  think for probably some of the  president's allies they're looking  forward to an opportunity for him again  to reset and to focus more on a unity  message getting back to the messages of  the bipartisan infrastructure bill right  bipartisan bringing republicans along on  the other hand for a lot of democrats  you've got a lot of frustration that  some of the core progressive agenda  items voting rights reform um some of  the more um you know expansive parts of  the build back better agenda are  probably not going to get passed in this  cycle of congress  but joel the two big parts of buildback  better that the president cares about a  lot he said are free community college  so the the country guaranteeing an  education two years beyond primary  school uh and also a child tax credit  which can lift millions of kids out of a  hunger uh if those are broken off and  passed does that change the narrative  for the midterms i mean it looks like  they're going to lose at the moment  could could could those bills those  separate pieces change that  well democrats have to decide whether or  not they want to tell a positive story  about this presidency if you look on one  hand you could say you passed a major  infrastructure bill you already passed  the big covet relief bill which had some  child care tax credit in it last year  and you've got a chance to pass a little  bit more going into 2022 and that's  something you could sell to voters as  you look at the midterm elections you  could you could tell the story like that  you could also tell the story in a way  to say we're not getting as much as we  want and i think what the president is  probably trying to do among many things  bringing the country together is also  bucking up his party and getting his  party to feel good about the work that  they've done over the last 13 months and  so i think part of the president's job  is psychological here to get his party  on board  right kane and hayes a political  consultancy you guys could work together  there's a little ring to that pain and  haze i like that there we go all right  we appreciate you guys thank you so much

